  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22410v2</id>
    <title>NeuroScalar: A Deep Learning Framework for Fast, Accurate, and In-the-Wild Cycle-Level Performance Prediction</title>
    <updated>2025-09-29T22:23:10Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22410v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22410v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The evaluation of new microprocessor designs is constrained by slow, cycle-accurate simulators that rely on unrepresentative benchmark traces. This paper introduces a novel deep learning framework for high-fidelity, ``in-the-wild'' simulation on production hardware. Our core contribution is a DL model trained on microarchitecture-independent features to predict cycle-level performance for hypothetical processor designs. This unique approach allows the model to be deployed on existing silicon to evaluate future hardware. We propose a complete system featuring a lightweight hardware trace collector and a principled sampling strategy to minimize user impact. This system achieves a simulation speed of 5 MIPS on a commodity GPU, imposing a mere 0.1% performance overhead. Furthermore, our co-designed Neutrino on-chip accelerator improves performance by 85x over the GPU. We demonstrate that this framework enables accurate performance analysis and large-scale hardware A/B testing on a massive scale using real-world applications.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-26T14:36:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Shayne and Yanxin co-first authors</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Shayne Wadle</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yanxin Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vikas Singh</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Karthikeyan Sankaralingam</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22407v3</id>
    <title>EMMA: Enhancing Robotic Control Robustness through a Generative Visual Model</title>
    <updated>2026-10-20T17:33:39Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22407v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22407v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The generalization of vision-language-action (VLA) models heavily relies on diverse training data. However, acquiring large-scale robot manipulation data across varied object appearances is costly and labor-intensive. To address this, we introduce Embodied Manipulation Media Adaptation (EMMA), a framework for augmenting VLA policies that combines a generative data engine with an effective training pipeline. We introduce DreamTransfer, a diffusion Transformer-based architecture for generating multi-view consistent and geometrically plausible embodied manipulation videos. It supports text-driven video editing on foreground, background and lighting while preserving 3D structure and geometric fidelity. We also utilize a hybrid training set of real and generated data and propose AdaMix to enhance the training process. AdaMix is a training strategy that adaptively weights samples according to policy performance to emphasize challenging samples. Evaluations show DreamTransfer outperforms previous video generation models in multi-view consistency, geometric fidelity, and text-conditioning precision. In real-world robotic tasks with zero-shot visual settings, our framework achieves a relative performance increase of over 92% compared to training with real data alone, and improves by an additional 17% with AdaMix.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-26T14:34:44Z</published>
